WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.480
Zac Efron got mega-jacked for "Baywatch,"&nbsp;
but he still managed to level up his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.400
physique for the upcoming wrestling&nbsp;
biopic "The Iron Claw." Here's how.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.040
For more than half a century with members spanning&nbsp;
three generations, the Von Erich family has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:22.360
legendary in the wrestling world, leading to&nbsp;
their induction into the WWE Hall of Fame in 2009.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.960
On hand to receive this prestigious honor&nbsp;
was Kevin Adkisson, aka Kevin Von Erich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.160
the last surviving son. The fascinating and tragic&nbsp;
story of Kevin and the rest of the Von Erich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.960
family is the subject of "The Iron Claw," in which&nbsp;
the famous wrestler is portrayed by Zac Efron.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.480
To play the part, Efron had to transform his body&nbsp;
in a massive effort that may be on par with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.320
he underwent in preparation for "Baywatch," which&nbsp;
is very impressive. However, while that experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.680
had its upsides, it also had significant downsides&nbsp;
that taught him what worked and what didn't.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.360
"For this movie in particular,&nbsp;
like, we wanted to achieve a look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.200
and that required a specific type of training."

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.000
The actor then took that knowledge and&nbsp;
used it to accomplish the new goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.960
increasing his bulk considerably&nbsp;
with great results. Let's take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.040
look at the vital steps the actor took&nbsp;
to pull off his latest formidable look.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.240
Efron was in incredibly great shape for his role&nbsp;
in "Baywatch," but to achieve those results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.960
the actor may have pushed himself&nbsp;
to a level exceeding his co-stars.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.280
At the time, Efron was all for putting&nbsp;
the work in, as he told Men's Journal,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.080
"There's this trigger that happens after two or&nbsp;
three weeks of dieting and eating healthy food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.560
where your body switches its primary energy source&nbsp;
from burning mainly carbohydrates to burning fat."

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.600
The actor relished the process because it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.160
make him crave foods he should be&nbsp;
eating (like sweet potatoes, beets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.960
and kale) instead of the junk that would&nbsp;
just make his goals harder to accomplish.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.240
On the other hand, the extreme steps it took to&nbsp;
maintain the chiseled abs of a Greek god were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.040
eventually not worth it. A few years after the&nbsp;
film was released, Efron admitted to Men's Health,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.800
"I started to develop insomnia, and I fell&nbsp;
into a pretty bad depression for a long time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.360
Something about that experience burned me&nbsp;
out. I had a really hard time recentering."

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.120
Despite the dark moments, Efron&nbsp;
learned a lot from that experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.880
and now fully understands the toll&nbsp;
it takes to have almost no body fat.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.360
"The more you learn, the more&nbsp;
your workouts evolve and change."

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.080
By far, the worst part of Efron's "Baywatch"&nbsp;
experience was the need to use pills called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.920
diuretics, which are usually intended&nbsp;
as a treatment for high blood pressure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.600
According to the Mayo Clinic, diuretics help&nbsp;
remove both sodium and water from your body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.640
an effect that is very useful when&nbsp;
trying to cut as much weight as possible.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.760
Yet this ended up doing more harm than&nbsp;
good, and was determined as the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.720
reason for Efron's descent into depression&nbsp;
during filming. As he told Men's Health,

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.640
"Ultimately, they chalked it up to taking&nbsp;
way too many diuretics for way too long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.760
and it messed something up."

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.240
Fortunately, to play Kevin Von Erich in&nbsp;
"The Iron Claw," none of that was needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.080
and the actor has preferred it&nbsp;
that way going forward. He added,

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.120
"That 'Baywatch' look, I don't know if&nbsp;
that's really attainable. There's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.120
too little water in the skin. Like, it's fake;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:22.200
it looks CGI'd. [...] I much prefer to have&nbsp;
an extra, you know, 2 to 3 percent body fat."

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.526
"I was not happy, not eating carbs."

00:03:25.526 --> 00:03:30.240
There are certainly aspects of Efron's intense&nbsp;
preparation for his "Baywatch" body that he will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.600
not do again for future projects, such as the&nbsp;
use of diuretics and some of the strict dietary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.200
choices. However, going into beast mode for&nbsp;
workouts is something that he doesn't regret.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.560
For the film, he trained two times a&nbsp;
day, five or six days out of the week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.920
but that didn't seem to bother&nbsp;
him. He explained to Men's Journal,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.200
"When I'm very careful about my&nbsp;
fitness and have a goal in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.200
it keeps me motivated and balanced.&nbsp;
I wake up earlier. I don't feel the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.680
need to be out — that kills your day,&nbsp;
your hobbies, and your motivation."

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.080
That same mentality came in handy for Efron as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.880
bulked up considerably for "The Iron&nbsp;
Claw." The actor told Men's Health,

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.760
"I enjoy pushing myself and&nbsp;
really laying it all out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.680
to the point where I kind of have to do&nbsp;
it. Otherwise, I don't feel like myself."

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.840
For his films since "Baywatch," Efron has&nbsp;
had a significantly healthier approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.320
to meals during shoots. In a diet-focused&nbsp;
exclusive for Men's Health, the actor said,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.280
"Sometimes when you're on set, I&nbsp;
give myself permission now to sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.400
of let up and eat what I need to eat.&nbsp;
It's not the time or the place to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.560
pressure on yourself unless you really&nbsp;
need to be shirtless that entire film."

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.320
The far less restrictive behavior has not only&nbsp;
allowed him to avoid a lot of stress, but those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.720
extra calories have also been essential for the&nbsp;
bulking up he needed to do for "The Iron Claw."

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.720
According to Healthline, the best way to&nbsp;
build muscle is to eat more food than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.000
body needs while carrying out intense&nbsp;
weightlifting. Based on the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.560
the added calories and Efron's vigor for&nbsp;
workouts were a highly effective combination.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.800
For some time, Efron's veganism helped him&nbsp;
achieve his lean build, but eventually he realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.720
that the restrictive diet was not great for his&nbsp;
health. With that in mind, the actor transitioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.720
to eating habits that worked better for his&nbsp;
body. When talking with Men's Health, he said,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.600
"I started intermittent fasting just&nbsp;
after I stopped being vegan. My body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.000
wasn't processing the vegetables in&nbsp;
the right way, so I decided to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.280
it and try something new. Intermittent&nbsp;
fasting's been really helpful with that."

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.200
Although it is possible to&nbsp;
be a vegan and still bulk up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.520
there is no denying that adding meats and&nbsp;
dairy products to the diet makes the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.280
considerably easier. According to Healthline,&nbsp;
bodybuilders rely on nutrient-dense foods high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:45.000
in protein and healthy fats. While the&nbsp;
Efron may not have known it at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.560
the switch turned out to be a great&nbsp;
decision in preparation for "The Iron Claw."

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:54.320
To show his commitment not just to bulking for&nbsp;
"The Iron Claw," but to using a protein-rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.320
diet to achieve that body goal, Efron met the&nbsp;
interviewer for Men's Health at a steakhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.960
in Thousand Oaks, California. The proof&nbsp;
that his veganism was long over came as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.040
he ordered a Wagyu steak and sides, along&nbsp;
with a seafood tower to start it all off.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.120
Efron then went on to explain that the&nbsp;
switch to focusing on protein-heavy foods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.040
made him feel significantly less&nbsp;
exhausted after intense workouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.040
but a part of him also wished that&nbsp;
was not the case. The actor admitted,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.320
"Morally, of course, I still wish I was vegan."

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.760
Yet at the same time, he also&nbsp;
seemed to have enjoyed the change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.840
based on the way he praised the Wagyu&nbsp;
steak once it arrived at the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.840
telling his interviewer, "that's&nbsp;
why I'm not vegan anymore."

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.840
For "Baywatch," Efron pushed his body to the&nbsp;
max and paid the price afterwards by throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:46.400
out his back, tearing his ACL, breaking&nbsp;
his wrist, and dislocating his shoulder.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.640
The actor learned his lesson the hard&nbsp;
way that if he was going into beast mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.240
he must also be just as extreme in his recovery.&nbsp;
Through his Instagram, Efron shares his extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:03.160
but successful solutions to relax his muscles,&nbsp;
like entering a cryotherapy chamber post-workout.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.640
But of all the methods that the actor adopted,&nbsp;
immersing himself in ice-cold water gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.960
the best results. He told Men's Health how ice&nbsp;
baths had become a vital part of his routine:

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:18.040
"It's my favorite part of the day.&nbsp;
Before is when it's most miserable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.960
and when you finally just commit and&nbsp;
jump in there. From that point forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.360
you've conquered something deep within&nbsp;
you; you do not want to get cold. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:31.440
the simplest philosophy: Anything you&nbsp;
don't want to do, make it a habit."

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.160
Given the high level of strain he had to&nbsp;
put his muscles through in the bodybuilding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:41.360
process for "The Iron Claw," it sounds like the&nbsp;
freezing-cold recovery is exactly what he needed.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.840
Almost every single day during the&nbsp;
production of "Baywatch," all Efron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:49.720
consumed was lean meat or fish with sweet&nbsp;
potatoes. Although the extremely limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:55.240
diet produced the desired results, he would&nbsp;
prefer not to go that route in the future.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.480
"That was the most extreme I ever got. I&nbsp;
think I was in ketosis that entire film."

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.360
To play Kevin Von Erich in "The Iron&nbsp;
Claw," Efron still relied on unprocessed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.000
nutrient-rich foods, but incorporated&nbsp;
a good amount of variety as well. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.840
talking with Men's Health, the actor laid&nbsp;
out his improved regimen in full, saying,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.080
"First meal of the day usually&nbsp;
starts with bone broth; I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.720
love bone broth. It's satisfying&nbsp;
and it's good for your stomach."

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:26.400
Moving on to something more substantial,&nbsp;
his next meal typically consisted of,

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.560
"Some sort of veggies with a&nbsp;
really clean source of protein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.440
whether it's chicken, beef,&nbsp;
venison, elk, anything."

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.960
Efron then ended with the most&nbsp;
calorie-dense part of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.080
to provide his muscles with the energy&nbsp;
needed to bulk up. He told Men's Health,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:45.520
"I usually have my highest carbohydrate&nbsp;
meal sort of in the evening. That's usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:50.640
another really good source of meat and healthy&nbsp;
carbohydrate like sweet potatoes or quinoa."

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.360
When an actor undergoes a major&nbsp;
body transformation for a role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.840
it's always great praise if the real-life person&nbsp;
they're supposed to play compliments their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:04.480
achievement. This is especially the case with Zac&nbsp;
Efron in "The Iron Claw," because Kevin Von Erich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.600
himself was thoroughly impressed with what he&nbsp;
has seen so far. The former wrestler told TMZ,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.960
"I saw a picture of the guy, he looked great.&nbsp;
I don't think I ever looked that good."

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.480
As an athlete himself, Kevin is&nbsp;
fully aware of what it took for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.600
Efron to bulk up like he did. The wrestler added,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.203
"He must really be working out hard.&nbsp;
[...] He does have a good body."

